[36m(TaskRunner pid=766)[0m 1. Melanie sold a third of her vacuum cleaners at the green house. So, she sold \( \frac{x}{3} \) vacuum cleaners at the green house. The number of vacuum cleaners left after this is:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=766)[0m    \[
[36m(TaskRunner pid=766)[0m    x - \frac{x}{3} = \frac{3x}{3} - \frac{x}{3} = \frac{2x}{3}
[36m(TaskRunner pid=766)[0m    \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=766)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=766)[0m 2. She then sold 2 more vacuum cleaners at the red house, so she sold \( \frac{2x}{3} + 2 \) vacuum cleaners at the red house. The number of vacuum cleaners left after this is:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=766)[0m    \[
[36m(TaskRunner pid=766)[0m    \frac{2x}{3} - \left( \frac{2x}{3} + 2 \right) = \frac{2x}{3} - \frac{2x}{3} - 2 = -2
[36m(TaskRunner pid=766)[0m    \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=766)[0m    This negative value indicates that she sold 2 more vacuum cleaners than she initially had, which is consistent with the problem setup. So, we can confirm that the number of vacuum cleaners left after the red house is:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=766)[0m    \[
[36m(TaskRunner pid=766)[0m    \frac{2x}{3} - 2
[36m(TaskRunner pid=766)[0m    \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=766)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=766)[0m 3. She then sold half of what was left at the orange house. So, she sold \( \frac{1}{2} \left( \frac{2x}{3} - 2 \right) = \frac{2x}{6} - 1 = \frac{x}{3} - 1 \) vacuum cleaners at the orange house. The number of vacuum cleaners left after this is:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=766)[0m    \[
